# TimelessMetrics Architecture

## Supervision Tree

The application starts a top-level `one_for_one` supervisor containing
`TimelessMetrics.Supervisor` (the data store) and `TimelessMetrics.HTTP` (the API server).
The data store supervisor uses a `rest_for_one` strategy so that if a foundational child
(like DB or SeriesRegistry) crashes, all children started after it are restarted in order.
Buffer and SegmentBuilder pairs are created per shard (default: `schedulers / 2`).

```mermaid
graph TD
    App["TimelessMetrics.Application<br/><i>one_for_one</i>"]
    App --> Sup
    App --> HTTP["TimelessMetrics.HTTP<br/><i>Rocket HTTP server</i>"]

    Sup["TimelessMetrics.Supervisor<br/><i>rest_for_one</i>"]

    Sup --> DB["TimelessMetrics.DB<br/><i>SQLite writer + reader pool</i>"]
    Sup --> SR["TimelessMetrics.SeriesRegistry<br/><i>persistent_term + ETS overflow</i>"]
    Sup --> DT["TimelessMetrics.DictTrainer<br/><i>zstd dictionary training</i>"]
    Sup --> SB0["SegmentBuilder 0<br/><i>compression worker</i>"]
    Sup --> BUF0["Buffer 0<br/><i>ETS write shard</i>"]
    Sup --> SBN["SegmentBuilder N<br/><i>compression worker</i>"]
    Sup --> BUFN["Buffer N<br/><i>ETS write shard</i>"]
    Sup --> Rollup["TimelessMetrics.Rollup<br/><i>periodic tier aggregation</i>"]
    Sup --> Ret["TimelessMetrics.Retention<br/><i>1-hr tick</i>"]
    Sup --> AE["TimelessMetrics.AlertEvaluator<br/><i>60-sec tick</i>"]
    Sup --> SelfMon["TimelessMetrics.SelfMonitor<br/><i>optional, default: on</i>"]
    Sup --> ScrapeSup["DynamicSupervisor<br/><i>:scrape_sup, one_for_one</i>"]
    Sup --> Scraper["TimelessMetrics.Scraper<br/><i>optional, default: on</i>"]

    ScrapeSup --> W1["Scraper.Worker<br/>target 1"]
    ScrapeSup --> W2["Scraper.Worker<br/>target 2"]

    style SelfMon stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style ScrapeSup stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style Scraper stroke-dasharray: 5 5
```

## Write Path

A write enters through `TimelessMetrics.write/4` which resolves the series via
`SeriesRegistry.get_or_create` — a lock-free path using persistent_term, ETS overflow,
and `:atomics` + `:ets.insert_new` for new series. The resolved series ID determines
which buffer shard receives the point via `rem(abs(series_id), shard_count)`.

The buffer shard is a `:public` ETS `ordered_set` with `write_concurrency: :auto`.
Writes are pure ETS inserts — no GenServer, no message passing, no blocking.

When the buffer reaches `flush_threshold` points or the periodic `flush_interval` fires,
all points are sent to the paired SegmentBuilder via `GenServer.cast` (non-blocking).
The SegmentBuilder accumulates points in memory grouped by `{series_id, time_window}`,
then periodically compresses dirty segments and writes them to disk.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    API["TimelessMetrics.write(store, metric, labels, value)"]
    API --> Resolve["SeriesRegistry.get_or_create"]

    subgraph "Series Resolution (lock-free)"
        Resolve --> PT[":persistent_term Map lookup"]
        PT -->|hit| SID["Return series_id"]
        PT -->|miss| ETS[":ets.lookup(overflow, key)"]
        ETS -->|hit| SID
        ETS -->|miss| CAS[":atomics.add_get + :ets.insert_new"]
        CAS --> SID
    end

    SID --> Route["rem(abs(series_id), shard_count)"]
    Route --> BUF[":ets.insert(shard_table,<br/>{{series_id, ts, seq}, value})"]

    subgraph "Buffer Flush (periodic or threshold)"
        BUF --> Flush["GenServer.cast(builder, {:ingest, grouped_points})"]
        Flush --> Accum["SegmentBuilder accumulates in memory"]
    end

    subgraph "Compression (async Task)"
        Accum --> Dirty{"Dirty segments<br/>since last flush?"}
        Dirty -->|yes| Compress["Gorilla + zstd (numeric)<br/>or RLE + zstd (text)"]
        Compress --> WAL["Write to WAL<br/>(data queryable)"]
        Dirty -->|no| Skip["No-op"]
    end

    subgraph "Sealing (periodic)"
        WAL --> Seal{"Time window<br/>completed?"}
        Seal -->|yes| Seg["Seal into immutable .seg file"]
        Seal -->|no| Keep["Keep in WAL"]
    end
```

**Batch writes** (`write_batch/2`) resolve all series IDs, group by shard, and call
`Buffer.write_bulk/2` per shard — one ETS insert per shard regardless of batch size.
For batches over 1,000 entries, resolution and shard routing parallelize across schedulers.

## Read / Query Path

Queries enter through `TimelessMetrics.query*/4` functions. The engine resolves the
series ID via `SeriesRegistry`, routes to the appropriate shard's `SegmentBuilder`, and
reads compressed segments from disk via lock-free `ShardStore` file operations.

For multi-series queries, `find_matching_series` reads from persistent_term + ETS overflow
(no SQLite on the hot path), groups matching series by shard to avoid cross-shard file
contention, and fans out in parallel via `Task.async_stream`.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Caller
    participant TM as TimelessMetrics
    participant SR as SeriesRegistry
    participant Q as Query
    participant SS as ShardStore (files)
    participant T as Task.async_stream

    C->>TM: query_aggregate_multi(store, metric, label_filter, opts)
    TM->>SR: find_matching_series (persistent_term + ETS)
    SR-->>TM: [{series_id, labels}, ...]

    TM->>TM: Group by shard

    alt Single shard
        TM->>Q: Query.aggregate(store, series_id, opts)
        Q->>SS: ShardStore.read_segments(series_id, from, to)
        SS-->>Q: compressed segment blobs
        Q->>Q: Decompress → filter → aggregate
        Q-->>TM: {:ok, [{bucket_ts, value}, ...]}
    else Multiple shards (fan-out)
        TM->>T: async_stream per shard group
        par Shard 0
            T->>Q: Query.aggregate per series
            Q->>SS: read_segments (shard 0 files)
        and Shard N
            T->>Q: Query.aggregate per series
            Q->>SS: read_segments (shard N files)
        end
        T-->>TM: [%{labels, data}, ...]
    end
    TM-->>C: {:ok, results}
```

## Storage Layer

Data lives in per-shard directories with immutable `.seg` files for completed time
windows and a WAL for pending data. SQLite stores metadata only — never raw time
series points. Numeric segments use Gorilla encoding + zstd. Text segments use
RLE + zstd (identified by a `0xFE` byte prefix).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph "Per-Shard Directory (shard_N/)"
        subgraph "raw/"
            WAL["current.wal<br/><i>pending compressed data</i>"]
            SEG1["1706000000.seg<br/><i>sealed time window</i>"]
            SEGN["1706014400.seg<br/><i>sealed time window</i>"]
        end
        subgraph "tier_hourly/"
            TH["chunks.dat + index.ets"]
        end
        subgraph "tier_daily/"
            TD["chunks.dat + index.ets"]
        end
    end

    subgraph "SQLite metrics.db"
        Series["series<br/>id, metric_name, labels, created_at"]
        Metadata["metric_metadata<br/>metric_name, type, unit, description"]
        AlertRules["alert_rules + alert_state + alert_history"]
        ScrapeTargets["scrape_targets + scrape_health"]
        Annotations["annotations<br/>id, timestamp, title, tags"]
    end

    BUF["Buffer"] -->|"flush"| SB["SegmentBuilder"]
    SB -->|"write pending"| WAL
    SB -->|"seal window"| SEG1
    SR["SeriesRegistry"] -->|"async batch INSERT"| Series
    Rollup -->|"write tier chunks"| TD
    Ret["Retention"] -->|"delete old .seg files"| SEG1
```

### Compression Pipeline

| Series Type | Stage 1 | Stage 2 | Measured |
|-------------|---------|---------|----------|
| Numeric | Gorilla (delta-of-delta + XOR) | zstd | 2.2 bytes/point |
| Text | RLE (run-length encoding) | zstd | Varies by cardinality |

## HTTP Ingest and Query

`TimelessMetrics.HTTP` is served by Rocket (custom HTTP server using OTP's `:socket`
module + picohttpparser NIF). Ingest endpoints accept VictoriaMetrics JSON lines and
Prometheus text exposition format — parsed via C++ NIF (Prometheus) or OTP 29's
built-in `:json.decode` NIF (JSON), then dispatched to sharded buffers.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Ingest
        VMJ["POST /api/v1/import<br/>VM JSON lines"]
        Prom["POST /api/v1/import/prometheus<br/>Prometheus text"]

        VMJ --> Parse["Parse → group by {metric, labels}"]
        Prom --> Parse
        Parse --> Resolve["SeriesRegistry → Buffer.write_bulk per shard"]
    end

    subgraph Query
        Exp["GET /api/v1/export<br/>raw points"]
        QLatest["GET /api/v1/query<br/>latest value"]
        QRange["GET /api/v1/query_range<br/>aggregated range"]
        PromQL["GET /prometheus/api/v1/query_range<br/>PromQL (Grafana)"]

        Exp --> QEng["Query.raw/aggregate → ShardStore reads"]
        QLatest --> QEng
        QRange --> QEng
        PromQL --> PQL["PromQL.parse → PromQL.execute"]
        PQL --> QEng
        QEng --> Resp["JSON response"]
    end

    subgraph Operational
        Health["GET /health<br/>Stats.snapshot + SeriesRegistry.count"]
        Backup["POST /api/v1/backup"]
        Metrics["GET /metrics<br/>Prometheus exposition"]
    end

    Auth["Bearer token check"] -.->|guards| Ingest
    Auth -.->|guards| Query
```

## Scraper Subsystem

The `Scraper` GenServer manages target CRUD and worker lifecycle. On init it loads
enabled targets from SQLite and starts a `Scraper.Worker` under `DynamicSupervisor`
for each. Workers schedule scrapes on fixed intervals with initial jitter to avoid
thundering herd. Each scrape tick fetches the target URL via HTTP, parses the
Prometheus exposition text, applies the metric relabel pipeline, and writes the
resulting entries through `TimelessMetrics.write_batch`. Workers track their own health
state and flush it to `scrape_health` periodically.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant API as HTTP API
    participant S as Scraper GenServer
    participant DB as SQLite
    participant DSup as DynamicSupervisor (:scrape_sup)
    participant W as Scraper.Worker
    participant Target as Scrape Target (HTTP)
    participant R as Relabel

    API->>S: add_target(params)
    S->>DB: INSERT INTO scrape_targets
    S->>DSup: start_child({Worker, target: t, store: s})
    DSup-->>S: {:ok, pid}

    Note over W: Initial scrape with random jitter
    W->>W: Process.send_after(:scrape, jitter)

    loop Every scrape_interval (default 30s)
        W->>Target: Req.get!(url, timeout: scrape_timeout)
        Target-->>W: Prometheus text body
        W->>W: Parse Prometheus text → [{metric, labels, value, ts}]
        W->>R: apply_configs(labels, metric_relabel_configs)
        R-->>W: relabeled entries (or :drop)
        W->>W: TimelessMetrics.write_batch(store, entries)
        W->>DB: Flush scrape_health (on state change or every 10 scrapes)
        W->>W: Reschedule :scrape on fixed anchor
    end
```

## Alert System

The `AlertEvaluator` GenServer ticks every 60 seconds and calls `Alert.evaluate/1`. For
each enabled rule, it queries recent metric data via `query_aggregate_multi`, then checks
the last bucket value against the rule's condition (`above`/`below` threshold). A per-series
state machine tracks transitions through `ok → pending → firing → resolved`. The `pending`
state implements configurable duration-based delays before firing. State changes to `firing`
or `resolved` trigger async webhook delivery and are logged to `alert_history`.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> ok
    ok --> pending: breaching AND duration > 0
    ok --> firing: breaching AND duration = 0
    ok --> ok: not breaching
    pending --> firing: breaching AND elapsed >= duration
    pending --> pending: breaching AND elapsed < duration
    pending --> ok: not breaching
    firing --> firing: still breaching
    firing --> resolved: not breaching
    resolved --> pending: breaching AND duration > 0
    resolved --> firing: breaching AND duration = 0
    resolved --> ok: not breaching

    note right of firing: webhook dispatched on entry
    note right of resolved: webhook dispatched on entry
```
